module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.PurelyInseparable.PerfectClosure
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPolynomial.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.FieldTheory.Separable.TFAE

@[expose] public section

/-!
# Polynomial approximations cannot be made regular

Stacks 07FH, clause (1), is formalized and unconditional as
`Stacks.Smoothing.exists_mvPolynomial_flat_localization`: for a field `k`, a Noetherian
geometrically regular `k`-algebra `Λ` and a prime `𝔮` of `Λ` it produces `m`, a `k`-algebra map
`φ : k[y_1, …, y_m] → Λ` and, with `𝔭 = φ^{-1}(𝔮)`, the two conclusions `𝔭 Λ_𝔮 = 𝔮 Λ_𝔮` and
`g : k[y]_𝔭 → Λ_𝔮` *flat*.

It was proposed to strengthen that clause by making `g` a *regular* homomorphism rather than
merely a flat one. Since `𝔭 Λ_𝔮 = 𝔮 Λ_𝔮` makes the closed fibre of `g` the single field
`κ(𝔮)`, and a field extension is geometrically regular exactly when it is separable, the
strengthening says precisely that `φ` can be chosen with `κ(𝔮)` separable over `κ(𝔭)`.

**This file proves that no such strengthening exists.** The obstruction is elementary and
absolute: `κ(𝔭)` is the subfield of `κ(𝔮)` generated by `k` together with the residues of
`φ(y_1), …, φ(y_m)`, so it is a *finitely generated* extension of `k`, whatever `φ` is; while
separability of `κ(𝔮)` over a subfield `F` forces every element of `κ(𝔮)` that is purely
inseparable over `k` to already lie in `F`. When the purely inseparable part of `κ(𝔮)/k` is
infinite, the two demands are incompatible for every `m` and every `φ`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Smoothing.perfectClosure_le_of_isSeparableExtension`: if `K` is separable over an
  intermediate field `F` of `K/k`, then the relative perfect closure of `k` in `K` — the elements
  of `K` purely inseparable over `k`, that is `k^{1/p^∞} ∩ K` — is contained in `F`. This is the
  obstruction in its sharpest form, and it is the necessary condition that the proposal
  overlooked.
* `Stacks.Smoothing.eq_top_of_isPurelyInseparable_of_isSeparableExtension`: if `K/k` is purely
  inseparable, the only intermediate field `F` with `K/F` separable is `K` itself.
* `Stacks.Smoothing.not_isSeparableExtension_adjoin_of_not_finiteDimensional`: if `K/k` is purely
  inseparable and infinite, then `K` is separable over no subfield generated over `k` by finitely
  many elements.
* `Stacks.Smoothing.not_isSeparableExtension_of_essFiniteType`: the same for every subextension
  that is essentially of finite type over `k`. The obstruction therefore kills every
  factorization `k → A → Λ_𝔮` with `A` essentially of finite type over `k` and `A → Λ_𝔮` regular,
  not only the polynomial shape of clause (1).
* `Stacks.Smoothing.not_isSeparableExtension_adjoin_range_of_not_finiteDimensional`: the same
  statement in the shape clause (1) presents it — for every `m` and every `k`-algebra map
  `ψ : k[y_1, …, y_m] → K`, the field generated by the image of `ψ` fails to separate `K`.
  `Stacks.Smoothing.adjoin_range_eq_adjoin_range_X` is the (elementary) identification of that
  field with `k` adjoined to the images of the variables, which is what makes it finitely
  generated.

Nothing here is tagged: these are not statements of a Stacks tag, they are the proof that one
proposed reading of 07FH, clause (1) is unavailable.

## The counterexample

The results above refute the proposal as soon as some Noetherian geometrically regular
`k`-algebra has a prime whose residue field has infinite purely inseparable part over `k`. One
does, and it is a complete discrete valuation ring — the shape to which Popescu's theorem is
actually applied.

Fix a prime `p`, let `k = 𝐅_p(t)` and let `K = ⋃_{n ≥ 0} 𝐅_p(t^{1/p^n})` be the perfect closure
of `k`. Let `Λ = K[[z]]`, a complete discrete valuation ring, and give it the `k`-algebra
structure sending `t` to `t + z`: for `0 ≠ f ∈ 𝐅_p[t]` the series `f(t + z)` has constant term
`f(t) ≠ 0` and so is a unit of `K[[z]]`, whence `𝐅_p[t] → Λ` extends to `k → Λ`. Take
`𝔮 = (z)`, so that `Λ_𝔮 = Λ` and `κ(𝔮) = K`.

*`Λ` is geometrically regular over `k`.* By Lemma 0381
(`Algebra.forall_isRegularRing_tensorProduct_essFiniteType_iff_finite_isPurelyInseparable`) it
suffices that `Λ ⊗_k k'` be regular for every finite purely inseparable extension `k'/k`. Since
`[k^{1/p} : k] = p`, the purely inseparable extensions of `k = 𝐅_p(t)` form the chain
`k_j = k(t^{1/p^j})`, and `k_j = k[x]/(x^{p^j} - t)`, so

  `Λ ⊗_k k_j = Λ[x]/(x^{p^j} - (t + z))`.

The element `t^{1/p^j}` lies in `K ⊆ Λ`; substituting `x = u + t^{1/p^j}` turns the relation into
`u^{p^j} - z`, because `(u + t^{1/p^j})^{p^j} = u^{p^j} + t`. Hence
`Λ ⊗_k k_j ≅ K[[z]][u]/(u^{p^j} - z) ≅ K[[u]]`, a discrete valuation ring, which is regular. (The
same computation read through `geometricallyRegular_tfae` says that the generator of
`H₁(L_{K/k})` goes to a generator of `𝔪/𝔪²`, which is the injectivity criterion of 07E5.)

*Clause (1) itself holds here, as it must.* Take `m = 1` and `φ(y) = t^{1/p^n}`. Then
`𝔭 = φ^{-1}(𝔮) = (y^{p^n} - t)`, the single generator maps to
`(t^{1/p^n})^{p^n} - (t + z) = -z`, so `𝔭 Λ_𝔮 = 𝔮 Λ_𝔮`, and `k[y]_𝔭 → Λ` is an injective local
homomorphism of discrete valuation rings, hence flat.

*But no `φ` makes `κ(𝔮)/κ(𝔭)` separable.* `K/k` is purely inseparable and `[K : k] = ∞`, and
`κ(𝔭)` is generated over `k` by the `m` residues of the `φ(y_i)`; so
`not_isSeparableExtension_adjoin_range_of_not_finiteDimensional` applies. Concretely the finitely
generated subextensions of `K/k` are exactly the `k_j`, and `K` contains `t^{1/p^{j+1}}`, which is
purely inseparable of degree `p` over `k_j` and so cannot lie in a separable extension of `k_j`.
Nothing changes if `φ` is allowed to land in `Λ_𝔮` rather than in `Λ`, or if the localized
polynomial ring is replaced by an arbitrary `k`-algebra essentially of finite type: `κ(𝔭)` is
generated over `k` by finitely many elements of `κ(𝔮)` either way, which is all the obstruction
uses.

## What the formalized clause (1) does give

The library's proof takes `φ = aeval mu` for `mu` a lift to `𝔮` of a regular system of parameters of
`Λ_𝔮`, and `comap_eq_span_range_X` then computes `𝔭 = (X_1, …, X_r)` on the nose. So in the
formalized clause (1) one has `κ(𝔭) = k` *identically*, and `Ω_{κ(𝔮)/κ(𝔭)} = Ω_{κ(𝔮)/k}`, which is
as far from vanishing as it can be. The strengthening would there read "`κ(𝔮)/k` is separable for
every prime of every geometrically regular `Λ`", and that is already false for `Λ = k[x]` over
`k = 𝐅_p(t)` at `𝔮 = (x^p - t)`, where `κ(𝔮) = k(t^{1/p})`. The formalized clause (1) therefore
carries no part of the strengthening, and the strengthening is not a corollary of it.

## The replacement that is available

What the differential mechanism of Lemma 07E6 does deliver, for a *prescribed* finite subset
`S ⊆ κ(𝔮)`, is a finitely generated `F` with `S ⊆ F ⊆ κ(𝔮)` such that `F` is finite *separable*
over `κ(𝔭)` — not that all of `κ(𝔮)` is separable over `κ(𝔭)`. Choosing `y_1, …, y_t ∈ F` whose
differentials form a basis of `Ω_{F/k}` makes `κ(𝔭) = k(y_1, …, y_t)` with `Ω_{F/κ(𝔭)} = 0`,
whence `F/κ(𝔭)` is finite separable by
`finite_and_isSeparable_of_subsingleton_kaehlerDifferential`; the further `dim Λ_𝔮 - δ(F)`
variables needed for `𝔭 Λ_𝔮 = 𝔮 Λ_𝔮` have residue zero and leave `κ(𝔭)` untouched, which is the
step `map_eq_and_flat_of_fibre` performs. In the counterexample this is visible: with `S ⊆ k_n`
one takes `F = k_n` and `y_1 = t^{1/p^n}`, and `κ(𝔭) = k_n = F`. That statement, and not
separability of `κ(𝔮)/κ(𝔭)`, is the correct target; it is what an essentially smooth
`k[y]_𝔭/𝔭^n → D` with residue field `F` requires, and it is consistent with the established
lesson that `D` must be allowed a residue field strictly larger than `κ(𝔭)` — larger, but
separably so.

